	'nw_all' => 'All notes from the clinic',
	'nw_h' => 'From the clinic',
	'pb_h' => 'Why balance fails, and why rest rarely finishes the job',
	'pb_p1' => 'Balance is a quiet agreement between the inner ear, the eyes and the feet. When one of them sends a poor signal, the others work harder and the world tilts: a spin on rolling over, a sway in a crowd, a lurch at a handrail in a bright corridor. The feeling is physical and specific. It is not a mood, and it is not fixed by being told to take it easy.',
	'pb_p2' => 'Rest helps in the first days of a vestibular hit, then it starts to work against you. The longer you avoid the bed-turn, the aisle, the tram platform or the screen, the less your brain practises the correction it needs. Avoidance keeps the symptom small in the moment and larger the next time you try. People arrive having waited months for it to pass on its own.',
	'pb_p3' => 'That is why the first hour here is a measurement, not a lecture. Gaze, position and the balance platform show which signal is off and which is compensating. From that we agree a short plan: a repositioning move if crystals are the cause, or retraining on the pad and in the corridor if the brain needs the practice. Home exercises fill the days between visits. Nothing in that plan is a promise. It is a way back to the street you already know.',
