	'pr_h' => 'Six fees, in euros',
	'pr_note' => 'Cancellation is free up to 24 hours before the visit. A receipt can be issued after you attend.',
	'prices_lead' => 'Fees at Taira Clinic are listed in euros. The figure you see is the fee for that visit. A receipt can be issued after you attend.',
	'prices_s1_h' => 'The six fees',
	'prices_s1_p1' => 'First assessment, 60 min, gaze, positional and balance-platform tests — €145. Rehabilitation session, 45 min — €85. Repositioning visit for BPPV, 30 min — €70. Six-session retraining block, prepaid — €460. Falls-risk screen for over-65s, 40 min — €60. Remote home-exercise review, 20 min — €35.',
	'prices_s1_p2' => 'A typical plan uses the first assessment and then sessions, often the prepaid block of six, over 8–12 weeks. Daily home exercises are part of that plan and are not billed as a separate visit unless you book the 20-minute remote review. The walking and turning test is part of clinical time already booked.',
	'prices_s1_p3' => 'It is not an extra line on this list. Ask the desk which fee applies before you come if your case might be a repositioning visit rather than a full assessment.',
	'prices_s2_h' => 'Receipts and insurance',
	'prices_s2_p1' => 'After a visit the desk can give you a receipt that names the service, the date and the fee you paid. If you hold insurance, take that receipt to your insurer and ask what your own policy repays for a vestibular assessment or a rehabilitation session.',
	'prices_s2_p2' => 'Taira Clinic cannot tell you what an insurer will cover, and the fees above are not reduced on the assumption that someone else will pay. Referrals from your own doctor are welcome. A referral is not a discount. Say on the booking form whether a doctor referred you so the letter at the end of the plan goes to the right person.',
	'prices_s2_p3' => 'Questions about a receipt go to desk@taira-clinic.example or +1 (555) 014-2200 during opening hours.',
	'prices_s3_h' => 'Can I cancel without a charge?',
	'prices_s3_p1' => 'Cancellation is free up to 24 hours before the appointment. Phone +1 (555) 014-2200 or email desk@taira-clinic.example with your name and the visit you need to move. First appointments are usually within 5 working days, so a cancelled assessment can be placed back on the list for that same kind of window.',
	'prices_s3_p2' => 'The desk is open Monday to Friday 08:00–18:30 and Saturday 08:30–13:00. Sunday and public holidays are closed. If you call outside those hours, leave a message and the name of the visit. The only cancellation rule we publish is the free window up to 24 hours before.',
	'prices_s3_p3' => 'Contact the desk if you are inside that window and need to talk about the time.',
	'prices_title' => 'Prices and cover',
	'privacy_lead' => 'Taira Clinic keeps the details you give us so we can book visits, provide care, and write to your own doctor when you have asked us to. This page says what those details are.',
	'privacy_s1_h' => 'What we record, and why',
	'privacy_s1_p1' => 'Taira Clinic is at 14 Linden Row, Harbourside, Northgate. You can reach the desk at desk@taira-clinic.example or +1 (555) 014-2200. When you book, we ask for your name, email, phone, date of birth, main symptom, how long the symptom has lasted, whether a doctor referred you, the days you prefer, a message, and your consent.',
	'privacy_s1_p2' => 'We use those details to arrange the 60-minute assessment and any later sessions, and to contact you about the time. If you have your own doctor, the path ends with a letter to that doctor about what we measured and what you practised. We send clinical information for that purpose, not for advertising.',
	'privacy_s1_p3' => 'Balance notes uses your email only if you send a request to join the monthly letter. We do not sell personal details.',
	'privacy_s2_h' => 'Balance notes, and how to ask about your details',
	'privacy_s2_p1' => 'Joining Balance notes is not the same as being booked. The page notice reads: Opens your email app. Send the message to join Balance notes; you are not subscribed yet. Until you send that message to desk@taira-clinic.example with the subject Balance notes request, we do not treat you as on the list. You can ask to be removed by emailing the desk.',
	'privacy_s2_p2' => 'You can also ask what we hold about you, and ask us to correct a name, a phone number or a doctor’s details. Write to desk@taira-clinic.example or call +1 (555) 014-2200 in opening hours, Monday to Friday 08:00–18:30 or Saturday 08:30–13:00. Daniel Achebe, the clinic manager, oversees the desk that handles those requests. We do not ask you to send passwords.',
	'privacy_s2_p3' => 'Emergency symptoms do not belong in a privacy email. Use your local emergency number for those.',
	'privacy_title' => 'Privacy',
	'sc1_t' => 'Mara, 64',
	'sc1_x' => 'Spinning after rolling over in bed, then a fall at a tram stop. A repositioning visit and six sessions over twelve weeks; back to walking to the market.',
	'sc2_t' => 'Theo, 31',
	'sc2_x' => 'Dizziness after a cycling crash. Eight sessions of post-concussion care; back on the bike on quiet roads first.',
	'sc3_t' => 'Ines, 47',
	'sc3_x' => 'Supermarket aisles and screens set off vertigo. Graded visual exposure; shopping with a list again.',
	'sc_h' => 'Three courses, told with permission',
	'sc_intro' => 'Each course is one person\'s. None is a forecast for the next patient.',
	'search_results' => 'Search results for: %s',
	'st1_l' => 'Opened',
	'st1_n' => '2011',
	'st2_l' => 'Clinicians',
	'st2_n' => '7',
	'st3_l' => 'First assessment',
	'st3_n' => '60 min',
	'st4_l' => 'Sessions in a typical plan',
	'st4_n' => '6',
	'stories_lead' => 'Three people agreed to have their courses told. Each course is one person\'s. Outcomes differ from person to person.',
	'stories_s1_h' => 'Mara, 64',
	'stories_s1_p1' => 'Mara was 64. Rolling over in bed set off a spin. She later fell at a tram stop, and a walk she knew stopped feeling safe. At Taira Clinic her care was a repositioning visit and then 6 sessions over 12 weeks. Between sessions she did 2 short home exercises a day. Twelve weeks on, she was walking to the market again.',
	'stories_s1_p2' => 'The repositioning visit we use for this pattern is 30 minutes. Her sessions sat inside the usual length of a plan, which is 6 sessions over 8–12 weeks. Her market walk is what she regained. It is not a result we offer to the next person who felt a spin in bed. Outcomes differ from person to person.',
	'stories_s2_h' => 'Theo, 31',
	'stories_s2_p1' => 'Theo was 31. Dizziness started after a cycling crash. That history sits with post-concussion care, which Jonah Lindqvist provides, after the first assessment has sorted what the dizziness is doing. Theo had 8 sessions. A typical plan here is 6, so his course was longer than the typical plan, and it is written that way on purpose.',
	'stories_s2_p2' => 'He got back on the bike on quiet roads first, not straight into traffic. The clinic did not treat the crash itself. We worked on the dizziness that followed it. If you have had a head injury and symptoms are worsening, or you have weakness, trouble speaking, or a sudden severe headache, use an emergency department before you use our diary.',
	'stories_s2_p3' => 'Outcomes differ from person to person.',
	'stories_s3_h' => 'Ines, 47',
	'stories_s3_p1' => 'Ines was 47. Supermarket aisles and screens set off her vertigo. The pattern is the one we call motion and visual vertigo: busy visual scenes, not only a spin when rolling over. Her plan used graded visual exposure, introduced in steps she could tolerate, rather than a single manoeuvre. She got back to shopping with a list again.',
	'stories_s3_p2' => 'The list mattered because it gave the aisle a task she had chosen. We do not publish a session count for Ines, and we will not invent one. Her story is here to show the kind of trigger and the kind of practice, not a timetable. Screens and aisles are worth naming when you book. Outcomes differ from person to person.',
	'stories_title' => 'Patient stories',
	'sx1_t' => 'Positional vertigo (BPPV)',
	'sx1_x' => 'A short, violent spin when you roll over, lie back or look up. A loose crystal in the inner ear is the usual cause, and a positional test shows it.',
	'sx2_t' => 'Vestibular neuritis',
	'sx2_x' => 'A long, hard attack of spinning with nausea, then unsteadiness that stays after the spinning eases. Retraining helps the brain rebalance.',
	'sx3_t' => 'Vestibular migraine',
	'sx3_x' => 'Dizziness with or without a headache, often worse with motion. We measure what the eyes and balance are doing before choosing a plan.',
	'sx4_t' => 'Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness',
	'sx4_x' => 'An ongoing sway or fog that is worse upright and in busy places. It often follows an earlier attack that has settled.',
	'sx5_t' => 'Post-concussion dizziness',
	'sx5_x' => 'Dizziness that stays after a head knock, with trouble in shops, on screens or when turning the head while walking.',
	'sx6_t' => 'Ménière\'s-type episodes',
	'sx6_x' => 'Spinning that arrives with ear fullness, a change in hearing or a noise in the ear, and then repeats.',
	'sx7_t' => 'Age-related unsteadiness',
	'sx7_x' => 'Slower rising from a chair, uneven pavements, near-falls. A falls-risk screen for over-65s takes 40 minutes.',
	'sx8_t' => 'Motion and visual vertigo',
	'sx8_x' => 'Supermarket aisles, scrolling screens and patterned floors set it off. Graded exposure retrains the response.',
	'sx_h' => 'Eight patterns we see',
	'sx_intro' => 'Each card names a pattern and what it feels like. The assessment decides which one is yours; the cards do not.',
	'sy1_t' => 'Spinning when rolling over',
	'sy1_x' => 'A turn in bed sets the room moving for seconds that feel much longer. Often a loose crystal in the inner ear, and often the reason for a short repositioning visit.',
	'sy2_t' => 'Unsteady in crowds',
	'sy2_x' => 'Stations, pavements and busy rooms make the ground feel unreliable. You stop, hold a pillar, and wait for the sway to settle before you move again.',
	'sy3_t' => 'Dizzy after a head knock',
	'sy3_x' => 'A fall from a bike, a knock in sport, a bump that did not seem serious at the time. The dizziness stays after the bruise has gone.',
	'sy4_t' => 'Falls or near-falls',
	'sy4_x' => 'Rising from a chair, a missed step, a hand that goes out too late. More common with age, and worth measuring before the next one lands.',
	'sy5_t' => 'Screens and aisles make you queasy',
	'sy5_x' => 'Supermarket rows, scrolling pages and patterned floors start a visual vertigo that rest on the sofa does not touch.',
	'sy_h' => 'What brings people in',
	'sy_intro' => 'People arrive with one clear problem. The cards name the five we see most, before any test is chosen.',
	'sy_more' => 'Read more',
	'sy_next' => 'Next cards',
	'sy_prev' => 'Previous cards',
	'symptoms_lead' => 'People come to Taira Clinic with a spin, a sway, or a street they have started to avoid. The first job is to name the pattern, which is what the symptom list and the 60-minute assessment are for.',
	'symptoms_s1_h' => 'Spinning, a long attack, or ear symptoms',
	'symptoms_s1_p1' => 'BPPV is the short spin set off by rolling over in bed, lying back, or looking up. It is a common reason people call the desk, and it is checked with a positional test. A repositioning visit is 30 minutes when that pattern is confirmed.',
	'symptoms_s1_p2' => 'Vestibular neuritis is different: a harder, longer attack of spinning, often with nausea, then unsteadiness that stays after the violent spinning eases. Ménière\'s-type episodes are the visits where spinning arrives with ear fullness, a change in hearing, or a noise in the ear, and then repeats. The clinic does not rename these from a phone call.',
	'symptoms_s1_p3' => 'The gaze test and the positional test are there so the label matches what your eyes and your history actually do.',
	'symptoms_s2_h' => 'Migraine, persistent dizziness, and visual motion',
	'symptoms_s2_p1' => 'Vestibular migraine can bring dizziness with or without a headache, and motion often makes it worse. Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness, or PPPD, is a different picture: an ongoing sway or fog, worse when you are upright and worse in places full of movement, such as a station concourse. It often follows an earlier attack that has already settled.',
	'symptoms_s2_p2' => 'Motion and visual vertigo is the pattern in which screens, scrolling shelves and supermarket aisles set the symptoms off. Ines, 47, came with that aisle-and-screen pattern. Her plan used graded visual exposure, and she got back to shopping with a list. Outcomes differ from person to person. If busy places are your main trigger, say so when you book.',
	'symptoms_s2_p3' => 'It changes which part of the assessment we lean on.',
	'symptoms_s3_h' => 'After a head knock, and when balance fades with age',
	'symptoms_s3_p1' => 'Post-concussion dizziness follows a head injury, such as a cycling crash. It may include trouble in shops, on screens, or when you turn your head while walking. Jonah Lindqvist sees people referred for post-concussion care. Theo was 31 when dizziness after a cycling crash brought him in. He had 8 sessions and got back on the bike on quiet roads first.',
	'symptoms_s3_p2' => 'Outcomes differ from person to person. Age-related unsteadiness is the slower loss of confidence: standing from a chair, uneven pavements, near-falls. Amara Osei works on falls prevention. People over 65 can book a falls-risk screen of 40 minutes. A near-fall counts. You do not need to have hit the ground before you ask for an assessment.',
	'symptoms_s4_h' => 'When should I go to an emergency department instead?',
	'symptoms_s4_p1' => 'Come to Taira Clinic for assessment and retraining. Do not come here first if the dizziness might be an emergency. Go to an emergency department, or call your local emergency number, if dizziness arrives with chest pain, fainting, a sudden severe headache, double vision, a drooping face, trouble speaking, or weakness in an arm or a leg.',
	'symptoms_s4_p2' => 'Go there too if symptoms follow a significant head injury and are getting worse. The clinic line, +1 (555) 014-2200, is for appointments. It is answered in opening hours only: Monday to Friday 08:00–18:30 and Saturday 08:30–13:00. We are closed on Sunday and public holidays.',
	'symptoms_s4_p3' => 'If you are unsure and the symptoms are sudden and severe, treat that as an emergency, not as a booking question.',
	'symptoms_title' => 'Symptoms we see',
	'tagline' => 'Vestibular & balance',
	'treatment_lead' => 'Treatment at Taira Clinic follows three stages: Measure, Retrain, Return. Sessions sit between a first assessment and a letter to your own doctor.',
	'treatment_s1_h' => 'Measure',
	'treatment_s1_p1' => 'Measure is the 60-minute first assessment. Gaze, positional testing and the balance platform are included, and the walking and turning test is added when we need to see you move. The fee is €145. Dr Lena Ortiz reads the history when a physician\'s view is needed before any exercise starts.',
	'treatment_s1_p2' => 'Measuring comes before a block of sessions because a spin from BPPV, unsteadiness after a head knock, and visual vertigo in an aisle do not use the same drills. You leave this stage knowing which findings matter and what we are not going to chase. The plan is agreed together.',
	'treatment_s1_p3' => 'If the tests point away from vestibular rehabilitation, we say that, and we still write to your own doctor rather than filling the diary.',
	'treatment_s2_h' => 'Retrain',
	'treatment_s2_p1' => 'Retrain is the hands-on stage. A rehabilitation session is 45 minutes and costs €85. A prepaid block of six retraining sessions is €460. Where the positional test shows BPPV, a repositioning visit is 30 minutes and costs €70. Foam-pad work is used when you need to practise standing with vision reduced or with the surface under you made unreliable.',
	'treatment_s2_p2' => 'A clinician spots you. They do not let go until the task is one you can hold. A typical plan is 6 sessions over 8–12 weeks. Theo\'s plan was 8 sessions after a cycling crash, which is his course, not the standard length.',
	'treatment_s2_p3' => 'Home exercises are set daily, and a remote home-exercise review is 20 minutes for €35 if you need a check between visits. Outcomes differ from person to person.',
	'treatment_s3_h' => 'Return',
	'treatment_s3_p1' => 'Return is the stage that moves the work out of the treatment room. You walk the corridor while turning your head, with a clinician alongside. Later the same idea is a kerb, a shop, or a quiet road, depending on what you stopped doing. Mara, 64, came back to walking to the market after repositioning and 6 sessions over 12 weeks.',
	'treatment_s3_p2' => 'Ines, 47, used graded visual exposure and shopped with a list again. Theo, 31, rode on quiet roads first. None of those endings is offered as a forecast. The review at the end of the plan is a conversation and a letter to your own doctor, which is the fifth step of the path.',
	'treatment_s3_p3' => 'We would rather you leave with a clear limit than with a slogan. Outcomes differ from person to person.',
	'treatment_s4_h' => 'The home exercise programme',
	'treatment_s4_p1' => 'Daily home exercises are part of step 4 of the path, alongside sessions, which are typically 6 over 8–12 weeks. The exercises are the ones practised in the room, written down before you leave, and kept short enough to repeat. Mara did 2 short home exercises a day during her 12 weeks. That number was hers.',
	'treatment_s4_p2' => 'Yours is agreed in the session, not copied from someone else\'s sheet. If an exercise stirs symptoms more than you and the clinician planned, stop and tell the desk. Do not add extra sets to hurry the plan. A remote review of 20 minutes, at €35, is there for checking the home work without a trip to Northgate.',
	'treatment_s4_p3' => 'Bring questions to the next session. The programme is finished at the review, not left open-ended.',
	'treatment_title' => 'Treatment',
